  geopolitics: "The transmission has acquired a second channel this weekend, and it runs the other way from the first. Channel one is unchanged and still bullish crude: Iran's campaign against Gulf shipping has kept Hormuz throughput at roughly 70% of the pre-conflict average in the week to October 14 on Kpler estimates, a ballistic missile aimed at Yanbu - a terminal whose whole strategic value is that it bypasses the strait - was intercepted on October 16 with no damage to oil and gas infrastructure, an Aramco administrative building in Dhahran was reportedly struck on October 14, the Houthis have declared a naval blockade of Saudi Arabia and struck Aden on October 17, and US commercial crude stocks fell 8.6 million barrels in the week to October 9 to about 3% below the five-year average with diesel still tight. Iran's diplomatic offer is also unchanged and still unanswered: Araghchi restated on October 18 that the conditions for reopening are an end to the American naval blockade, the lifting of sanctions on Iranian oil and a ceasefire on all fronts, with the strait reopening on day six and negotiations beginning on day seven. Trump rejected that plan in September and has since announced what CBS reported as a crushing economic operation against Iran, while US Central Command says the vast majority of attempted attacks on shipping are unsuccessful and the waterway remains open for transit. Nothing there has improved. Channel two is new and it is bearish crude, and the point for a trader is that it does not require Tehran's consent. Trump signed an executive order on Friday October 16, released over the weekend, waiving the Graham Act's new Russia sanctions and tariffs two days before they were due to take effect - including tariffs of up to 100% on the five largest buyers of Russian crude and gas, which plausibly means China and India - while the Treasury separately waived sanctions on Russian diesel sales until April 2027. Existing sanctions under earlier executive orders remain. Lawmakers in both parties and President Zelensky condemned it; the administration called it a very good decision and tied it to a diesel purchase. For the oil market the politics are beside the point: this is an administration choosing to put more crude and more distillate into a tight market by signature, five days before the November 3 midterms, with gasoline up 86.33% year to date. The FX read follows directly. First, the crude premium now has a policy off-switch as well as a diplomatic one, and policy moves faster - which is why USOIL comes off LONG to RANGE and why the Canadian dollar, while holding plus 2 on Macklem's October 17 escalation, carries a smaller correlated book today: AUD/CAD and CAD/JPY both come down to LOW-MED. Second, the euro area is the board's largest energy importer and gets no relief at all, because its problem is gas rather than crude: EU gas is plus 214.49% year to date and German gas plus 197.42%, both still rising on the week, so a 3.8% HICP stays a terms-of-trade tax on a currency whose central bank has said it is not seeing second-round effects. Third, the haven read is the subtle one and it argues against pressing the metals shorts. In the week to Friday, which delivered an oil shock, a shooting war, an AI credit scare and an IMF inflation upgrade, gold fell 0.66% and silver 6.27% while the Swiss franc gained 1.23% against the dollar and the US ten-year fell about 24 basis points. The haven bid existed - it simply went into currencies and Treasuries rather than metals. That is why XAU/USD goes to RANGE rather than staying SHORT, why XAG/USD comes down to LOW-MED, and why four of this file's range calls sit on the franc: a currency with a hard policy cap at zero and a genuine haven bid is a levels trade, not a trend. Fourth, copper is the one commodity whose story is physical rather than geopolitical - six-week-low LME stocks at 233,025 tonnes, a 97 dollar cash premium to three-month, an El Teniente mudslide, an Antofagasta strike projected to cut November output about 50%, Chinese import premiums at a four-year high - and that is why XCU goes to MEDIUM and why it, rather than crude, is the better terms-of-trade leg behind the Australian dollar today.",
